VALUES ('947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 'aws-saa-practice-4', 'AWS SAA-C03 Practice Paper 4', 'aws-saa', ARRAY['IAM Identity Center', 'GuardDuty', 'Session Manager', 'ACM', 'SNS Fan-out', 'Step Functions', 'Kinesis', 'Graviton', 'Storage Lens']::TEXT[], 20, 'standard', true, 4, 30);

-- ── Domain: Design Secure Architectures (30% — 6 questions) ──

VALUES ('d6ca7678-e7ea-4b2d-ab19-cb1ead0d658a', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 1, 'A company with 20 AWS accounts under AWS Organizations wants employees to log in ONCE using their existing corporate directory credentials (e.g., Active Directory or a third-party identity provider), then access whichever specific accounts and roles they''re authorized for — without maintaining separate IAM users in every individual account. What AWS service is purpose-built for this?');
VALUES ('d6ca7678-e7ea-4b2d-ab19-cb1ead0d658a', 'f5a8a814-e83b-431d-9e62-64c4cda54e13', 'IAM Identity Center is AWS''s purpose-built service for exactly this: centralized workforce identity and single sign-on across every account in an AWS Organization. It integrates with external identity providers (Active Directory, Okta, Azure AD, etc.) via SAML, or can use its own built-in identity store, and lets administrators assign users/groups to specific "permission sets" scoped to specific accounts — an employee logs in once and sees only the accounts/roles they''re actually authorized for, with the underlying temporary credentials (via STS, covered in Paper 3) handled transparently. Manually replicating IAM users across 20 accounts is exactly the unscalable, hard-to-audit anti-pattern this service exists to eliminate. Cognito is designed for CUSTOMER-facing application authentication (end users of an app you build), not internal AWS account access for employees/administrators — a common category-confusion distractor.');

VALUES ('ba68fdfb-0a03-4450-b19e-5bd652832706', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 2, 'A company wants continuous, automated threat detection across their AWS accounts — identifying things like unusual API calls from a known-malicious IP, an EC2 instance communicating with a cryptocurrency-mining pool, or credentials being used from an anomalous geographic location — without deploying or managing any agents on their resources. Which AWS service provides this?');
VALUES ('ba68fdfb-0a03-4450-b19e-5bd652832706', '281e33aa-859a-427b-b821-ab53698e8ccb', 'GuardDuty is a managed threat detection service that analyzes existing data sources — VPC Flow Logs, DNS query logs, CloudTrail management and S3 data events — against continuously updated threat intelligence feeds and machine learning models, flagging findings like traffic to/from known-malicious IPs, cryptocurrency-mining behavior signatures, or anomalous credential usage patterns (like an API call from a geographic region the account has never operated from). Critically, it requires NO agents to install or manage on EC2 instances or anywhere else — it works purely from log/event analysis, which is a key distinguishing feature over agent-based security tools. AWS Config tracks resource CONFIGURATION state/compliance (covered in Paper 3), not behavioral threat detection. Amazon Inspector scans for known VULNERABILITIES in EC2/container/Lambda workloads (missing patches, CVEs) — a point-in-time/periodic assessment, not continuous behavioral network threat detection the way GuardDuty is.');

VALUES ('3b9c9196-9957-4cd0-9e46-33fb78705098', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 3, 'A security team uses GuardDuty, Inspector, and Config across multiple accounts, but finds it tedious to check each service''s console separately to get an overall security posture view. What AWS service aggregates findings from these (and other) security services into a single, unified dashboard with an overall security score?');
VALUES ('3b9c9196-9957-4cd0-9e46-33fb78705098', '387353be-36a8-4160-b4f1-d66fd86e3613', 'Security Hub is specifically designed as the aggregation layer sitting above individual security services — it ingests and normalizes findings from GuardDuty (threat detection), Inspector (vulnerability scanning), Config (configuration compliance), IAM Access Analyzer, and others into one consistent finding format and a single dashboard, avoiding the need to check each service''s console separately. It also runs its own automated checks against recognized security standards (like the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark or AWS Foundational Security Best Practices) and produces an overall security score to track posture over time. CloudTrail logs API activity/management events (covered in earlier papers) — it is a DATA SOURCE other services consume, not itself an aggregation dashboard for security findings. Macie is a specific data-classification/PII-discovery service (scanning S3 for sensitive data) — a narrower tool, not a cross-service aggregator.');

VALUES ('0f080658-1496-4715-bf70-e72567c796f8', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 4, 'An EC2 instance in a private subnet needs to be occasionally accessed by administrators for troubleshooting, but the company wants to avoid opening inbound port 22 (SSH) on any Security Group, avoid managing SSH key pairs, and avoid running a bastion host. What AWS Systems Manager feature enables secure shell access under these constraints?');
VALUES ('0f080658-1496-4715-bf70-e72567c796f8', 'c545ebac-dbfc-4cdd-ac81-81f2c37f0495', 'Session Manager (a capability of AWS Systems Manager) lets an authorized IAM principal open an interactive shell session to a managed instance entirely through the SSM Agent making an OUTBOUND connection to the Systems Manager service — this means no inbound port needs to be opened on the instance''s Security Group at all (port 22 can remain fully closed), no SSH key pairs need to be generated, distributed, or rotated, and no bastion/jump host needs to be built and maintained as an additional piece of exposed infrastructure. Session activity can also be logged to S3 or CloudWatch Logs for audit purposes, which manual direct SSH access typically does not provide as cleanly. This is a genuinely real, commonly recommended, and frequently tested pattern specifically because it eliminates several real attack surfaces (open SSH port, key management, bastion host) simultaneously. Config and Macie serve entirely different functions (configuration compliance and data classification respectively) with no shell-access capability at all.');

VALUES ('7c255fae-e1dd-4363-a9bf-f8fae584dd75', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 5, 'A company needs a TLS/SSL certificate for their public website served through an Application Load Balancer, and wants the certificate to renew automatically before expiration without manual intervention. What is the correct AWS approach?');
VALUES ('7c255fae-e1dd-4363-a9bf-f8fae584dd75', '477bb206-441e-4a63-b8f8-0d5185634bda', 'ACM issues and manages public (and private) TLS/SSL certificates, and for certificates used with integrated AWS services — an ALB, CloudFront, API Gateway — ACM handles automatic renewal before expiration with no manual re-issuance or re-upload step required, as long as the domain validation ACM originally used remains in place (e.g., a DNS validation CNAME record staying in the domain''s DNS zone). This directly eliminates a common, real-world operational risk: a certificate silently expiring because a manual renewal process was missed, causing a public-facing site to suddenly show browser security warnings. Manually managing third-party certificates and re-uploading them yearly is exactly the error-prone, manual-intervention-dependent process ACM exists to remove. KMS manages encryption KEYS for data encryption (covered in Paper 1) — a genuinely different service and purpose from TLS certificate issuance/management, a common category-confusion distractor. TLS certificates absolutely do expire (commonly after 13 months or less for publicly trusted certificates) — automatic renewal is a real, meaningful operational concern.');

VALUES ('30b49ff4-fd67-4d06-912c-31457a962a57', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 6, 'An S3 bucket policy grants read access to a specific external AWS account (Account B), but a user in Account B still receives "Access Denied" when attempting to read an object, even though their own IAM policy in Account B allows s3:GetObject on that bucket. What is the most likely explanation?');
VALUES ('30b49ff4-fd67-4d06-912c-31457a962a57', '5ac8b744-dd06-4717-9f39-5676c43cdb47', 'Cross-account S3 access genuinely requires permission at two layers: the RESOURCE-based policy (the bucket owner''s bucket policy, or an object ACL) must explicitly grant the external account/user access, AND the requesting user''s OWN account IAM policy must also allow the S3 action — missing either one results in Access Denied. This two-sided requirement (similar in spirit to the KMS cross-account pattern from Paper 1) is a very common real-world troubleshooting scenario: an administrator updates the bucket policy but forgets that the requesting user''s own IAM policy in their home account also needs an explicit allow, or vice versa. IAM policies within the requester''s own account are absolutely still evaluated for cross-account resource access — they are not ignored; both the resource-based and identity-based sides of the permission model apply simultaneously. Re-uploading an object has no bearing on policy evaluation, and bucket policies do genuinely take effect when correctly configured — the issue in this scenario is that only one of the two required sides was actually granted.');

-- ── Domain: Design Resilient Architectures (26% — 5 questions) ──

VALUES ('9d15d8e9-ec44-4dd6-b7c2-28030574639a', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 7, 'A global application runs identical, fully functional deployments in both us-east-1 and eu-west-1, both actively serving live traffic simultaneously (not one primary/one standby). Route 53 should route each user to whichever of the two Regions currently responds fastest for them. What Route 53 routing policy achieves this?');
VALUES ('9d15d8e9-ec44-4dd6-b7c2-28030574639a', '5c3cd53e-14d9-40fa-9ef9-31a2e76d7d2a', 'Latency-based routing directs each DNS query to the Region (among the records configured) that offers the lowest network latency for that specific requester, based on AWS''s continuously measured latency data between AWS Regions and various points on the internet — this fits a genuine multi-Region ACTIVE-ACTIVE architecture (both Regions live and serving real traffic to whichever users are closer/faster to reach) directly, unlike Failover routing (covered in Paper 2), which is specifically designed for a PRIMARY/STANDBY relationship — only the primary serves traffic under normal conditions, with the secondary only activating on a health-check failure, not simultaneously alongside the primary. Simple routing just returns one (or a random one of several) configured value with no health-check or latency awareness at all — it does not perform any latency comparison. Route 53 latency-based routing is a real, commonly used, and frequently tested feature specifically for this active-active use case.');

VALUES ('86661f9c-e5da-49c9-903b-45a4664a2a00', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 8, 'An Application Load Balancer distributes traffic across instances registered in three Availability Zones, but two AZs have significantly more registered instances than the third. Without cross-zone load balancing, what problem occurs, and how does enabling it help?');
VALUES ('86661f9c-e5da-49c9-903b-45a4664a2a00', 'e2717762-f07d-4f21-ab05-1fe26b42decc', 'By default, an ELB distributes incoming traffic evenly ACROSS the enabled Availability Zones first, and only then evenly among the instances WITHIN each AZ — this means if AZ-A has 8 instances and AZ-B has 2 instances, each AZ still receives roughly the same total share of traffic, so each of AZ-B''s 2 instances individually receives a much larger share of requests than each of AZ-A''s 8 instances, an uneven per-instance load despite the ALB "working correctly" at the AZ level. Cross-zone load balancing changes this behavior to distribute traffic evenly across ALL registered instances, treating them as one pool regardless of which AZ each is in — directly correcting the per-instance imbalance described. (Note: for Application Load Balancers specifically, cross-zone load balancing is enabled by default and cannot be disabled — this question tests understanding of WHY the feature exists and what it corrects, a concept that also applies to Network Load Balancers, where it is NOT on by default.) It has a real, direct effect on traffic distribution — it is not merely a billing consideration, and ALBs absolutely support distributing traffic across multiple AZs as their fundamental purpose.');

VALUES ('94874a74-a57d-4152-9867-b92859cf7fa5', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 9, 'An order-processing event needs to simultaneously trigger THREE independent downstream actions — updating inventory, sending a confirmation email, and logging to an analytics pipeline — each processed by a separate, independent consumer. Using a single SQS queue would mean only ONE consumer could process each message. What messaging pattern and AWS service correctly fan a single event out to multiple independent subscribers?');
VALUES ('94874a74-a57d-4152-9867-b92859cf7fa5', 'd011db7d-03b5-47b6-8ef2-ff68bcab8ef6', 'The SNS fan-out pattern is the standard, purpose-built solution: publish the event once to an SNS topic, and subscribe multiple SQS queues (or Lambda functions, or other supported subscribers) to that SAME topic — SNS then delivers an independent copy of the message to EVERY subscriber, so the inventory-update consumer, the email consumer, and the analytics consumer each receive and process their own copy without competing for or consuming each other''s message. This is the direct fix for SQS''s own default behavior described correctly in the premise: a standard SQS queue delivers each individual message to only ONE consumer that successfully processes and deletes it (this is what makes plain SQS suited to distributing WORK across a pool of identical workers, not broadcasting the SAME event to multiple DIFFERENT consumer types) — SQS alone does not multicast a message to several different consumers, which is exactly why SNS''s fan-out pattern combining SNS (broadcast) with per-consumer SQS queues (reliable, individually-buffered delivery to each) is the standard architecture for this need. Direct Connect is a dedicated network connectivity service, entirely unrelated to message fan-out.');

VALUES ('6d9549cb-dc3d-4a8b-b10c-9e6031005f47', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 10, 'A company wants to deploy a new, potentially risky RDS engine version upgrade with the ability to fully test the new version against production-like traffic before committing, and to switch back near-instantly if a problem is discovered, minimizing downtime risk compared to an in-place upgrade. What RDS deployment approach fits this?');
VALUES ('6d9549cb-dc3d-4a8b-b10c-9e6031005f47', 'f83b6215-7fa0-4af8-9637-0cf9c855bc6e', 'RDS Blue/Green Deployments create a full, synchronized copy ("green") of the production database ("blue") running the new engine version (or other change), keeping them in sync via replication while the green environment can be independently tested against realistic traffic patterns — once validated, RDS performs a fast, minimal-downtime switchover redirecting the application to the green environment, with the original blue environment retained immediately afterward as a fast rollback option if a problem is discovered post-switchover. This directly matches "test before committing" and "switch back near-instantly" far better than a direct in-place upgrade, which applies changes directly to the single production instance with no separate testing environment and a harder, slower rollback path if something goes wrong. Multi-AZ (covered in Paper 1) provides failover HA between an active primary and a synced standby — it is not designed as an upgrade-testing/validation mechanism the way Blue/Green Deployments specifically are, a genuine distinction in purpose despite both involving a second synchronized copy of the database.');

VALUES ('75b6bcaa-d356-4b8b-9667-5efe698cc88c', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 11, 'A company takes EBS snapshots of a 500GB volume daily. After the first full snapshot, each subsequent daily snapshot completes in a fraction of the time and consumes far less storage than a full 500GB copy would suggest. Why?');
VALUES ('75b6bcaa-d356-4b8b-9667-5efe698cc88c', '08f31a26-99c1-4a24-a523-68c1a389499c', 'EBS snapshots are incremental by design: the first snapshot of a volume captures every block (a full copy), but every SUBSEQUENT snapshot of that same volume only needs to capture the blocks that have changed since the previous snapshot — unchanged blocks are simply referenced from the earlier snapshot rather than copied again. This is precisely why later snapshots complete faster and consume less additional storage than the volume''s total size might suggest, proportional to how much data has actually changed. This incremental design also matters for restoring: when restoring ANY snapshot (not just the most recent), EBS transparently reconstructs the full volume by combining the relevant blocks across the snapshot chain — the user does not need to understand or manage this chain manually. No data is deleted or emptied as part of this process; the efficiency comes entirely from only storing genuinely changed data each time.');

-- ── Domain: Design High-Performing Architectures (24% — 5 questions) ──

VALUES ('8f362693-23ed-4ca8-8e58-992b50c0163c', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 12, 'A multi-step business process (validate order → charge payment → update inventory → send confirmation) is currently implemented as one large Lambda function calling each step sequentially in code, making it hard to visualize progress, retry individual failed steps, or handle steps that legitimately take hours (like waiting for manual approval). What AWS service is designed to orchestrate exactly this kind of multi-step, stateful workflow?');
VALUES ('8f362693-23ed-4ca8-8e58-992b50c0163c', '2448e90e-b695-4fb8-96b2-16704c27648c', 'Step Functions is purpose-built for orchestrating exactly this kind of multi-step, potentially long-running, stateful workflow — you define the workflow as a state machine (using Amazon States Language), where each state can invoke a Lambda function, another AWS service, wait for a callback (supporting genuinely long waits like a manual approval step, well beyond a single Lambda''s maximum 15-minute execution limit), or branch into parallel paths. It provides built-in visual execution history (seeing exactly which step succeeded/failed/is in progress), and per-step retry and error-handling configuration, all without needing to hand-write that orchestration logic (and its failure-handling edge cases) inside one large, harder-to-observe Lambda function. SQS is a message queue (covered elsewhere) — a building block sometimes used WITHIN a workflow, but not itself a workflow orchestration/state-machine service. CloudFormation is an infrastructure-as-code provisioning tool (deploying AWS resources), an entirely different function from runtime application business-process orchestration.');

VALUES ('88f57a79-be12-4503-aed6-b24d10b8186f', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 13, 'A REST API built on API Gateway and Lambda serves the same, relatively static GET request (e.g., a product catalog listing) thousands of times per minute, with the underlying data changing only every few minutes. Each request currently invokes Lambda and queries the database fresh. What API Gateway feature would reduce this redundant backend load?');
VALUES ('88f57a79-be12-4503-aed6-b24d10b8186f', '0601f2e6-d029-4514-adf7-e30bd4c9345d', 'API Gateway supports a built-in response caching feature: once enabled with a configured TTL (time-to-live), identical requests within that window are served directly from the API Gateway cache, without invoking the backend integration (Lambda, or any other backend type) at all — directly matching this scenario''s repeated, relatively static GET request pattern, and directly reducing Lambda invocation count, database query load, and response latency for cached requests. This is a real, commonly used API Gateway feature specifically for exactly this "same data requested very frequently, changes rarely" pattern. X-Ray provides distributed tracing/observability (seeing how a request flows through services) — an entirely different function from caching. WAF filters malicious traffic (covered in Paper 2) — also unrelated to response caching.');

VALUES ('93de47d5-90e6-4a37-ad12-ef3d25c7f5f8', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 14, 'A company operates thousands of IoT sensors, each sending a small telemetry reading every second, and needs to ingest this continuous, high-volume stream of data in real time for immediate processing (not batch), with the ability to have multiple independent applications consume the same stream. Which AWS service is purpose-built for this?');
VALUES ('93de47d5-90e6-4a37-ad12-ef3d25c7f5f8', '7a7d7648-9a0d-49bc-a28a-f3f6950b69e1', 'Kinesis Data Streams is purpose-built for real-time ingestion and processing of high-volume, continuous data streams — exactly matching thousands of IoT sensors each streaming small readings continuously. Data is organized into shards (each providing a defined read/write throughput capacity, scalable by adjusting shard count), and — critically for this scenario — data remains available in the stream for a configurable retention window (24 hours by default, extendable), during which MULTIPLE INDEPENDENT consumer applications can each read through the same stream data independently, at their own pace, without consuming or removing it for other consumers (a genuinely different model from SQS, where a message is typically consumed once and removed). Glacier is a COLD, archival storage class (covered in Paper 1) — the functional opposite of real-time, sub-second data ingestion. Snowball is for large-volume, OFFLINE, physical-device data migration (covered in Paper 3) — also the opposite of continuous, real-time, online streaming ingestion.');

VALUES ('941b1713-f08a-4db1-aaf6-9c6063001a64', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 15, 'A company runs general-purpose, compute-intensive workloads on EC2 and wants to reduce cost while maintaining or improving performance, without any application code changes required for compatible workloads. Which EC2 processor family option is specifically designed to offer better price-performance for many general workloads?');
VALUES ('941b1713-f08a-4db1-aaf6-9c6063001a64', 'a312be91-ca71-4415-88c7-22196ac2e9a4', 'Graviton processors are AWS''s own custom-designed ARM-based chips, offered across several instance families, and AWS has published benchmarks claiming meaningfully better price-performance than comparable x86 (Intel/AMD) instances for many common workload types. Because Graviton uses a different processor architecture (ARM, not x86), truly "no code changes" compatibility depends on the workload: software already compiled for ARM (or written in a language that compiles cleanly to ARM, or using multi-architecture container images) can often move with minimal friction, while workloads with hard x86-specific dependencies need rebuilding and testing before migrating — a real, testable nuance, not a blanket zero-effort switch for every workload. T-family (burstable, covered in Paper 1) is explicitly NOT suited to sustained compute-intensive work — its burst credits deplete under sustained load, the opposite fit for this scenario. D-family targets high-density sequential storage throughput (covered in Paper 1), not general compute price-performance.');

VALUES ('a26fdde0-63f3-477c-83e0-f78b9904b868', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 16, 'A reporting dashboard queries an RDS read replica for near-real-time business metrics. Occasionally, a metric shown on the dashboard is a few seconds behind the very latest write to the primary database. Is this expected behavior, and why?');
VALUES ('a26fdde0-63f3-477c-83e0-f78b9904b868', '94a2a5cf-3c53-4183-87ab-2a2861f9dd90', 'RDS Read Replicas replicate from the primary ASYNCHRONOUSLY — the primary does not wait for the replica to confirm it has received/applied a write before considering that write committed, which is precisely why replicas can be used for read scaling without slowing down write performance on the primary, but it also means there is normally some small replication lag (often sub-second, but genuinely not guaranteed to be zero, and can grow under heavy write load or replica resource constraints). This is a real, expected, architecturally inherent characteristic of asynchronous replication, not a failure — and is the direct contrast to Multi-AZ''s standby (covered in Paper 1), which historically has used more synchronous-style replication specifically for its failover/HA guarantee, a different design goal than read-scaling. A reporting dashboard tolerating a few seconds of staleness in exchange for offloading read traffic from the primary is a completely standard, reasonable architectural trade-off, and understanding WHY that staleness is expected (not a bug) is the actual tested concept.');

-- ── Domain: Design Cost-Optimized Architectures (20% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('14664ffa-b4b3-4242-8ed6-da8b2bd9f273', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 17, 'A company has a genuinely steady-state EC2 workload on one specific instance family and Region for the next 3 years, and separately has other flexible workloads spread across changing instance types/Regions. For MAXIMUM possible discount specifically on the steady-state workload (accepting less flexibility in exchange), which purchasing option should they choose over a Savings Plan?');
VALUES ('14664ffa-b4b3-4242-8ed6-da8b2bd9f273', 'f5ee68fd-ebe4-429a-bee9-3c30d37246fa', 'Standard Reserved Instances, particularly with a longer term (3-year) and upfront payment, generally offer the deepest discount available among EC2 purchasing options — but they trade away the flexibility Savings Plans and Convertible RIs provide (Standard RIs are tied to a specific instance family and Region, and cannot be easily changed if the workload''s needs shift). This trade-off is genuinely worth making specifically when a workload is TRULY steady-state and unlikely to change instance family or Region over the commitment period — exactly the scenario described — since the "cost" of reduced flexibility is effectively zero for a workload that was never going to change anyway, while the discount is typically the deepest available. For the OTHER flexible workloads mentioned in the question, a Savings Plan (covered in Paper 1) would be the better fit precisely because THEY do change instance type/Region. On-Demand is the highest-cost, most flexible option, the opposite of a discount-maximizing choice, and Spot is designed for interruptible, fault-tolerant workloads (covered in Paper 1) — a poor fit for guaranteed steady-state production capacity that cannot tolerate reclamation.');

VALUES ('3336e180-e30c-4784-b484-02fe611778a1', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 18, 'A company has hundreds of S3 buckets across dozens of accounts in an AWS Organization and wants a single, organization-wide view of storage usage, cost, and activity trends, along with actionable recommendations to reduce cost (e.g., identifying buckets that would benefit from Lifecycle policies). What AWS service provides this?');
VALUES ('3336e180-e30c-4784-b484-02fe611778a1', '727693e0-c8e5-4d6f-a5ae-989d7b4b66d4', 'S3 Storage Lens is specifically built for organization-wide S3 visibility — aggregating storage usage, activity, and cost-related metrics across every account and bucket in an AWS Organization into dashboards, with a "free" default tier and a more detailed paid tier, and it surfaces specific, actionable recommendations (like identifying buckets with significant incomplete multipart uploads wasting storage, or buckets that would benefit from Lifecycle policies based on their actual access patterns) — directly matching "single organization-wide view" and "actionable cost-reduction recommendations." Macie (covered in Paper 3) is a data-classification/PII-discovery service — an entirely different function from storage cost/usage analytics. AWS Config tracks resource CONFIGURATION state/compliance (covered in Paper 3), not storage usage/cost analytics. This IS a real, purpose-built AWS capability specifically because checking hundreds of buckets individually does not scale operationally.');

VALUES ('d4489f60-1f93-46ed-9cd5-da090ff96bd3', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 19, 'A cost review discovers several EBS volumes that are not attached to any running EC2 instance (their instances were terminated, but the "delete on termination" setting was not enabled for these volumes), yet the company is still being billed for them monthly. What is the correct explanation and fix?');
VALUES ('d4489f60-1f93-46ed-9cd5-da090ff96bd3', 'e96a5b97-fe32-41da-9d8d-2552e685471b', 'EBS volumes are billed based on provisioned size (and IOPS/throughput for certain volume types) REGARDLESS of whether they are currently attached to, or in use by, a running instance — an unattached volume left over after its instance was terminated (because "delete on termination" was not enabled, or the volume was deliberately detached and simply forgotten) continues to accrue storage charges indefinitely, with no automatic expiration or deletion. This is a genuinely common, real-world cost leak — similar in shape to the unattached Elastic IP billing scenario from Paper 2, but for EBS storage specifically — and the fix is either verifying the volume is genuinely no longer needed and deleting it, or (proactively, for future instances) ensuring "delete on termination" is appropriately configured so root/temporary volumes don''t silently outlive their instance. EBS provides no automatic time-based self-deletion of orphaned volumes under any default configuration.');

VALUES ('8b782934-3b55-4d80-839d-175ecf0e02f0', '947fe1e1-2c0a-4532-98ec-b561776bf202', 20, 'A company wants a fully managed way to identify EC2 instances, EBS volumes, and RDS instances that are idle or significantly underutilized across their entire AWS account, as a starting point for cost reduction, without building custom CloudWatch metric analysis themselves. Besides AWS Compute Optimizer (covered previously), which broader AWS service also surfaces this kind of underutilized-resource finding, alongside security and performance recommendations, in one place?');
VALUES ('8b782934-3b55-4d80-839d-175ecf0e02f0', '1610ca7f-53e0-4b73-bc5f-86afd15dc368', 'Trusted Advisor evaluates an AWS account against best practices across several categories, and its Cost Optimization category specifically flags exactly this kind of finding — low-utilization EC2 instances, unattached/idle EBS volumes (directly relevant to the previous question in this same paper), idle RDS instances with no recent connections, underutilized Redshift clusters, and similar — giving a starting checklist for cost reduction without building custom analysis. This sits alongside Trusted Advisor''s other categories (Security, Performance, Fault Tolerance, Service Limits) in one consolidated view, distinct from Compute Optimizer''s narrower, deeper focus specifically on RIGHT-SIZING recommendations for compute resources (covered in Paper 2) — the two tools are complementary, not identical, with Trusted Advisor casting a broader, more general best-practices net. Direct Connect (dedicated network connectivity) and Route 53 (DNS) both serve entirely unrelated functions with no resource-utilization-analysis capability of their own.');
